WEBVTT

1
00:00:17.074 --> 00:00:21.916
Medlock's story sounded like something in a book, and it did not make Mary feel happy.

2
00:00:22.996 --> 00:00:27.298
A house with a hundred rooms and nearly all of them shut up with their doors locked!

3
00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:31.040
A man with a crooked back who shut himself up too!

4
00:00:32.136 --> 00:00:36.460
Mary pressed her lips together and stared out the train window as it began to rain.

5
00:00:40.083 --> 00:00:41.344
expect to see Mr.

6
00:00:41.384 --> 00:00:44.447
Craven because you probably won't," said Mrs.

7
00:00:44.507 --> 00:00:49.711
Medlock. "And you must not expect that there will be people to talk to you.

8
00:00:50.652 --> 00:00:54.295
You'll have to play by yourself and look after yourself.

9
00:00:55.416 --> 00:01:00.040
You'll be told what rooms you can go into and what rooms you're to keep out of.

10
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.780
There are many gardens.

11
00:01:03.341 --> 00:01:06.681
But when you're in the house, don't go wandering or poking about.

12
00:01:07.662 --> 00:01:09.542
Mr. Craven won't stand for that."

13
00:01:09.562 --> 00:01:14.604
"I won't want to go poking about," said Mary in a sour voice.

14
00:01:15.704 --> 00:01:17.164
She had felt sorry for Mr.

15
00:01:17.204 --> 00:01:21.545
Craven, but now she felt he deserved all the trouble that had come his way.

16
00:01:22.566 --> 00:01:24.266
He certainly sounded unpleasant.

17
00:01:25.787 --> 00:01:27.147
Mary turned away from Mrs.

18
00:01:27.207 --> 00:01:30.468
Medlock and watched the rain streaming down the window of the train.

19
00:01:31.632 --> 00:01:32.733
Soon she was asleep.

20
00:01:33.774 --> 00:01:36.035
When Mary woke up, it was quite dark.

21
00:01:37.116 --> 00:01:38.997
The train had stopped, and Mrs.

22
00:01:39.057 --> 00:01:40.318
Medlock was shaking her.

23
00:01:40.338 --> 00:01:42.360
"Open your eyes!

24
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.142
We're at Thwaite Station, and we have a long drive ahead of us."

25
00:01:47.703 --> 00:01:49.625
Mary stood up and waited while Mrs.

26
00:01:49.685 --> 00:01:51.186
Medlock collected their things.

27
00:01:52.187 --> 00:01:55.269
They stepped down from the train and found a carriage waiting for them.

28
00:01:56.309 --> 00:01:57.730
A footman opened the door.

29
00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:02.545
After they were seated, he climbed up next to the driver, and they set off.

30
00:02:05.586 --> 00:02:09.268
Mary was not a shy child, and she was not exactly frightened.

31
00:02:10.208 --> 00:02:14.770
But there was no knowing what might happen in this strange house on the edge of the moor.

32
00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.811
"What is a moor?"

33
00:02:17.411 --> 00:02:18.772
she suddenly asked Mrs.

34
00:02:18.832 --> 00:02:24.074
Medlock. "If you look out the window in about ten minutes, you'll see one.

35
00:02:25.074 --> 00:02:28.876
You won't see much because it's a dark night, but you can see something.

36
00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.782
We have to drive eight kilometers across Missel Moor before we get to the manor."

37
00:02:36.243 --> 00:02:41.205
Mary asked no more questions but waited in the darkness, keeping her eyes on the window.

38
00:02:42.205 --> 00:02:48.428
They drove through a tiny village, where Mary saw a church, a few shops, and some whitewashed cottages.

39
00:02:49.548 --> 00:02:53.130
Soon they were on the highway, and she saw hedges and trees.

40
00:02:54.671 --> 00:02:57.492
After a while the horses began to go more slowly.

41
00:02:58.528 --> 00:03:00.289
There were no more hedges or trees.

42
00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:06.112
Mary could see nothing but a dense darkness beyond the yellow light of the carriage lamps.

43
00:03:06.132 --> 00:03:10.755
"We're on the moor now, sure enough," said Mrs.

44
00:03:10.815 --> 00:03:16.038
Medlock. The rough road seemed to cut through bushes and low-growing things.

45
00:03:17.118 --> 00:03:19.740
The wind began to make a wild, rushing sound.

46
00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.742
"It's it's not the sea, is it?"

47
00:03:24.423 --> 00:03:26.204
asked Mary, looking at Mrs.

48
00:03:26.264 --> 00:03:28.721
Medlock. "It sounds just like the sea."

49
00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:32.844
"That's the wind blowing through the bushes," Mrs.

50
00:03:32.924 --> 00:03:33.625
Medlock said.

51
00:03:33.645 --> 00:03:40.690
"The moor is a dreary place, though there are plenty of people who like it, especially when the

52
00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:41.851
heather is in bloom.

53
00:03:43.031 --> 00:03:46.053
Nothing lives on it but wild ponies and sheep."

54
00:03:47.695 --> 00:03:49.936
On and on they drove through the darkness.

55
00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:54.820
The rain stopped, but the wind whistled and made strange sounds.

56
00:03:55.932 --> 00:03:59.914
Mary felt as if the drive—and the moor—would never end.

57
00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:03.456
"I don't like it," she said to herself.

58
00:04:04.637 --> 00:04:07.058
She pinched her lips tightly together for a moment.

59
00:04:07.078 --> 00:04:08.499
"I don't like it."

60
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.061
The horses climbed a hilly part of the road.

61
00:04:13.062 --> 00:04:15.143
Just then Mary caught sight of a light.

62
00:04:16.183 --> 00:04:19.145
Mrs. Medlock saw it too and gave a sigh of relief.

63
00:04:19.165 --> 00:04:22.927
"I am so glad to see that bit of light twinkling!"

64
00:04:23.427 --> 00:04:24.148
she exclaimed.

65
00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.621
"We shall soon get a good cup of tea."

66
00:04:29.042 --> 00:04:35.166
But after the carriage passed through the gates of the estate, there were still three more kilometers to

67
00:04:35.206 --> 00:04:40.509
drive. At last they arrived at Misselthwaite Manor.

68
00:04:41.529 --> 00:04:45.051
When they stepped down from the carriage, they were standing in a courtyard.

69
00:04:46.072 --> 00:04:47.733
A servant opened the front door.

70
00:04:49.254 --> 00:04:52.956
As Mary walked into the enormous hall, she felt very small.

71
00:04:54.044 --> 00:04:56.325
A thin, old man stood near the servant.

72
00:04:57.426 --> 00:05:00.627
"You are to take her to her room," he said.

73
00:05:00.647 --> 00:05:03.709
"Mr. Craven doesn't want to see her.

74
00:05:04.109 --> 00:05:05.950
He's going to London in the morning."

75
00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.751
"Very well, Mr.

76
00:05:08.791 --> 00:05:10.312
Pitcher," said Mrs.

77
00:05:10.372 --> 00:05:14.234
Medlock. "Come along," she said to Mary.

78
00:05:15.294 --> 00:05:19.076
She led Mary up a broad staircase and down a long corridor.

79
00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.283
Then they went up a short flight of steps and through two more corridors.

80
00:05:25.404 --> 00:05:30.267
Finally they came to a room with a fire in the fireplace and supper on a table.

81
00:05:32.328 --> 00:05:33.709
"Well, here you are!"

82
00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:34.770
said Mrs.

83
00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:40.974
Medlock. "This room and the next are where you'll live—and you must not wander from them.

84
00:05:42.055 --> 00:05:43.196
Don't forget that!"

85
00:05:44.397 --> 00:05:45.457
Mary looked around.

86
00:05:45.477 --> 00:05:48.980
"I don't like it," she said to herself again.
